WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.188
The core defensive idea is simple:

2
00:00:02.268 --> 00:00:05.529
the application chooses the storage root
and the business object;

3
00:00:05.609 --> 00:00:09.161
user input must not be allowed to redirect
the operation somewhere else.

4
00:00:09.241 --> 00:00:12.094
Treat user-controlled path segments as
untrusted input.

5
00:00:12.174 --> 00:00:15.609
Avoid passing user-supplied paths directly
to Java filesystem APIs.

6
00:00:15.689 --> 00:00:17.494
When dynamic selection is required,

7
00:00:17.574 --> 00:00:21.708
prefer application-controlled IDs mapped
to metadata records over raw path input.

8
00:00:21.788 --> 00:00:23.942
When filesystem paths must be constructed,

9
00:00:24.022 --> 00:00:27.049
start from a fixed, application-controlled
base directory.

10
00:00:27.129 --> 00:00:31.205
Resolve the candidate against that base
and verify the final result remains inside

11
00:00:31.285 --> 00:00:34.604
the intended boundary before reading,
writing, deleting, moving,

12
00:00:34.684 --> 00:00:35.674
or serving the file.

13
00:00:35.754 --> 00:00:39.596
Reject absolute paths, parent-directory
movement, unexpected separators,

14
00:00:39.676 --> 00:00:42.413
empty names, reserved names, unsupported
characters,

15
00:00:42.493 --> 00:00:45.171
and platform-specific surprises where
appropriate.

16
00:00:45.251 --> 00:00:49.210
Keep the accepted input format narrow
enough that reviewers can reason about it.

17
00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:52.609
Be careful with transformations that
happen before the path check.

18
00:00:52.689 --> 00:00:56.532
URL decoding, repeated decoding, Unicode
normalization, mixed separators,

19
00:00:56.612 --> 00:00:59.057
case handling, symbolic links, archive
entries,

20
00:00:59.137 --> 00:01:01.582
and mounted volumes can change how a path
behaves.

21
00:01:01.662 --> 00:01:05.680
Object storage keys have similar boundary
problems even though they are not local

22
00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:06.691
filesystem paths.

23
00:01:06.771 --> 00:01:08.576
Prefixes, tenant IDs, object names,

24
00:01:08.656 --> 00:01:12.848
and generated keys should be controlled
and validated before they influence storage

25
00:01:12.928 --> 00:01:13.452
behavior.

26
00:01:13.532 --> 00:01:17.084
Path-boundary checks must be paired with
object-level authorization.

27
00:01:17.164 --> 00:01:21.182
A resolved path can be inside the correct
base directory and still point to another

28
00:01:21.262 --> 00:01:23.590
user's report or another tenant's
attachment.

29
00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:27.455
A storage key can be in the right bucket
and still represent the wrong business

30
00:01:27.535 --> 00:01:27.943
object.

31
00:01:28.023 --> 00:01:31.341
Preventing traversal keeps the operation
inside the storage area;

32
00:01:31.421 --> 00:01:34.740
authorization decides whether this caller
may access this object.
